# d5-02 — configuration judgement, knowledge curation, collaboration
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project holds meeting minutes going back two years, and questions are almost always about the last quarter.|Keep the recent minutes and archive the rest elsewhere|Keep everything, since an older meeting could occasionally be relevant and having the full history available means those questions can still be answered when they arise|Add an instruction to prefer recent minutes|Reorganise the minutes by topic rather than by date so that the relevant material is easier to locate|Curating to the material questions actually use keeps answers precise. Two years of unused minutes compete with the quarter that matters.|Keeping everything for rare cases dilutes the common ones. A preference instruction relies on correct application, and reorganising does not reduce the volume.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|An associate's project instruction tells the model to always ask a clarifying question before answering, and colleagues find it slow.|Scope the clarifying question to cases where the request is genuinely ambiguous|Remove the instruction so that answers come back immediately|Keep the instruction exactly as it is|Ask colleagues to provide more detail in their initial requests|An unconditional clarification rule fires on requests that need no clarification. Scoping it to ambiguity keeps the benefit without the friction.|Removing it loses the benefit on genuinely ambiguous requests. Keeping it ignores the complaint, and asking users to pre-empt it shifts the burden.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team wants to know whether a document should stay in their Project, and it has not been referenced in any answer for four months.|Remove it, and restore it if a question later shows it was needed|Keep it, since four months without a reference does not mean it will never be relevant to a question somebody asks at some point in the future|Keep it in place but mark it as being low priority|Ask the team whether anyone expects to need it, and keep it if any of them says that they might do so at some stage in their work|Unused knowledge costs precision on every question. Removing with the option to restore makes curation reversible and low-risk.|Keeping against hypothetical need is how knowledge bases bloat. Priority marking does not reduce the material, and canvassing for maybes finds a reason to keep everything.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team shares a Project with a partner organisation and wants to be sure nothing confidential is exposed.|Review what the Project holds against what the partner may see, before sharing|Add an instruction telling the model not to disclose confidential information to external users who are accessing the Project through the sharing arrangement|Share it and monitor the questions that get asked|Ask the partner to agree not to ask questions that would require confidential information to be included in the answers they receive from the Project|Sharing exposes the Project's contents to its audience. Auditing what it holds against what the audience may see is the control that does not depend on runtime behaviour.|Output instructions rely on correct classification every time. Monitoring detects after disclosure, and a partner agreement does not restrict what the system can produce.
organisation|5.4|3|An associate has Projects that are each used by one person and Projects used by the whole team, with no way to tell them apart.|Adopt a convention that distinguishes shared Projects from personal ones|Ask people to check with the team before changing any Project in case it turns out to be one that other people are also relying on for their work|Make every one of the Projects a shared Project|Keep a list recording which Projects are shared and which are personal|The distinction matters for how changes are made, so it needs to be visible at the point of use. A naming convention carries it.|Pre-change checks depend on remembering. Making everything shared removes personal space, and an external list is another artefact to maintain.
projects-setup|5.1|3|A team is configuring a Project and wants the model to refuse questions outside its scope.|State the scope and instruct it to decline and say why when asked outside it|Assume the model will recognise when a question falls outside the Project's purpose based on the documents that have been uploaded to it|List out the topics that it should refuse to answer|Add an instruction telling the model to be cautious about answering questions that seem to fall outside what the Project was designed to handle|Refusal behaviour needs the boundary stated and the response defined. Saying why the question is out of scope is what makes the refusal useful.|Assuming recognition leaves the boundary implicit. An exclusion list cannot be exhaustive, and vague caution produces hedged answers rather than clear refusals.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team's Project answers have degraded since they doubled the number of documents, and all the documents are relevant.|Check whether the questions need all of them, or split into focused Projects|Accept the degradation as the cost of having comprehensive knowledge available within a single Project that covers the whole of the team's subject area|Move to a model with a larger context window|Ask users to be more specific in their questions so that the relevant documents are easier to identify within the larger body of material|Relevance to the subject is not the same as relevance to each question. Splitting into focused Projects restores precision where broad coverage was diluting it.|Accepting degradation forgoes a fixable problem. A larger window accommodates rather than resolves, and user specificity works around the structure.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|An associate uploads a document to a Project and answers do not appear to reflect it at all.|Check the document uploaded and processed correctly before changing anything else|Rewrite the questions so that they refer more directly to the material contained in the document that has recently been added to the Project|Upload the document again in case it failed|Add an instruction telling the model to consult the newly uploaded document when answering questions that relate to the topics it covers|When new knowledge has no effect, the first question is whether it is actually present and readable. That is checkable before any other change.|Rewording, re-uploading and instructing all assume the document is there and usable, which is the assumption to test first.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team's project instructions include a rule that was added to fix a problem that no longer occurs.|Remove it, since standing instructions apply to everything and cost attention|Keep it in case the problem recurs, since removing it would mean the issue could come back|Keep it but note that it may be obsolete|Move it to the end of the instructions so that it has less influence|Every instruction consumes attention that other instructions need. A rule addressing a resolved problem is a permanent cost for no benefit.|Retaining against recurrence pays indefinitely. A note does not reduce the cost, and repositioning keeps it in effect.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team's Project knowledge is accurate but written for an audience with more expertise than the people now asking questions.|Add material pitched at the current audience, or instruct the model to translate|Keep the material as it is, since it is accurate and the model can be relied upon to explain technical content in accessible terms when it is asked to do so|Ask users to look up terms they do not understand|Replace the material with simpler versions so that everything in the Project is pitched at the level of the people who are currently using it|A mismatch between source register and audience is addressed by adding accessible material or instructing translation, both of which preserve the accurate source.|Assuming automatic translation without instruction is unreliable. Asking users to look things up shifts the burden, and replacing accurate material risks losing precision.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|An associate wants colleagues to use their Project but not to change how it is configured.|Share it in a way that grants use without configuration rights|Ask colleagues not to change the instructions, since they should have no reason to modify a configuration that has been set up for the purpose they are using it for|Keep the Project private and run requests for them|Take a copy of the configuration so that it can be restored if a colleague changes something that affects how the Project behaves for other users|Access levels are what enforce the distinction between using and editing. Sharing at the right level makes the constraint structural.|A request relies on compliance. Running requests for people is a bottleneck, and a restorable copy is recovery rather than prevention.
organisation|5.4|3|A team has one Project that has grown to serve four unrelated purposes because it was the first one anyone created.|Split it along the purposes so each has its own scope and knowledge|Keep it as a single Project and use clear instructions describing which behaviour applies to each of the four different purposes it currently serves|Keep the single Project and simply rename it|Add documents covering all four purposes more thoroughly|Four unrelated purposes in one Project means every question sees all four sets of context. Splitting restores the isolation each purpose needs.|Conditional instructions must be resolved correctly every time. Renaming changes the label, and adding material makes the dilution worse.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team's Project includes a document that is mostly irrelevant but contains one crucial paragraph.|Extract the paragraph and include that rather than the whole document|Include the whole document, since the crucial paragraph needs its surrounding context in order to be interpreted correctly by anyone reading it|Include the document and highlight the paragraph|Summarise the document so that the crucial paragraph is retained alongside a condensed version of everything else it happens to contain|Curating to what matters keeps the signal strong. One crucial paragraph does not justify carrying a document of irrelevant material on every question.|Including everything for context dilutes it. Highlighting relies on the emphasis being honoured, and summarising still carries the irrelevant material in compressed form.
projects-setup|5.1|3|An associate is setting up a Project and cannot decide whether to make it broad or narrow in scope.|Make it narrow, since narrow scope produces precise answers and Projects are cheap to create|Make it broad so that it can handle a wide range of questions|Make it broad initially and narrow it later|Make its scope match the department's remit so that the boundary is meaningful to the organisation and easy for people to reason about when they use it|Narrow scope keeps context relevant to every question, and creating another Project is cheap. That trade favours precision.|Broad scope dilutes every answer. Narrowing later means untangling accumulated material, and organisational remit is not the same as question coherence.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project answers cite a document accurately but the document itself contains an error nobody has noticed.|Recognise that knowledge quality bounds answer quality, and verify the sources|Improve the instructions so the model checks claims before including them in the answers it produces for the team's questions|Add a second document that contradicts the first one|Ask the model to indicate when it is uncertain about a claim|A Project cannot be more accurate than what it holds. Verifying the source material is the only thing that addresses an error in the knowledge itself.|Instructions cannot check facts against a source the model does not have. A contradicting document creates conflict, and uncertainty flags do not detect a confidently wrong source.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team wants their Project to produce output in a fixed structure, and roughly one response in ten deviates.|State the structure precisely and validate responses before they are used|Strengthen the wording of the instruction until the deviations stop occurring in the responses that the Project produces for the team|Ask users to reformat the responses that deviate|Add an instruction telling the model to check its own output against the required structure before returning it to the person who asked the question|Instruction plus validation is the durable pattern. The instruction lowers the rate and the check handles what gets through, which no wording alone eliminates.|Stronger wording chases an asymptote. Manual reformatting repeats forever, and self-checking inside the same response is not independent.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|An associate finds their Project contains three versions of the same document uploaded at different times.|Keep the current version and remove the others|Keep all three so that the history of how the document has changed over time remains available to anyone who needs to understand its evolution|Rename the three versions by their upload date|Add an instruction telling the model to use the most recent of the three versions|Multiple versions of the same document produce inconsistent answers. Keeping only the current one is what makes the Project's knowledge unambiguous.|Version history belongs elsewhere. Date naming relies on correct selection, and a recency instruction has the same dependency.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team's shared Project is used by people in two departments who need slightly different things from it.|Check whether the difference is large enough to warrant separate Projects|Add instructions covering both departments' needs so that the single Project serves each of them appropriately|Ask each department to specify their department in their questions|Configure it for the larger department, since that covers the majority of the usage and the other department can adapt their questions accordingly|Whether one Project can serve both depends on how far the requirements diverge. Assessing that is the question before choosing a mechanism.|Conditional instructions and per-question department flags both presume one Project is right. Configuring for the majority ignores the other department's needs.
organisation|5.4|3|An associate wants to find a Project they set up eight months ago and remembers only roughly what it was for.|Improve the naming going forward so this does not recur|Search each Project's contents until the right one is found|Recreate the Project from scratch instead|Ask colleagues whether they remember the Project being set up and what it might have been called at the time it was originally created|The immediate search is unavoidable, but the durable answer is a naming convention that makes Projects findable by what they do.|Searching solves today and leaves the pattern. Recreating duplicates work, and asking colleagues about a personal Project is unlikely to help.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team wants to know why their Project sometimes misses information that is definitely in one of its documents.|Ask questions that name the topic more specifically so retrieval has more to work with|Upload the document again so that it is processed freshly and the information becomes available to the model when it answers future questions|Reduce the total number of documents held|Add an instruction telling the model to search all documents thoroughly before concluding that information is not available in the Project's knowledge|Retrieval over a body of documents depends on how well the question matches the material. Naming the topic specifically is the lever you control.|Re-uploading addresses a processing failure that is not indicated. Reducing documents is a blunt fix, and thoroughness instructions do not change how matching works.
projects-setup|5.1|3|A team is configuring a Project and wants to prevent people from using it for work it is not suitable for.|State what it is for and what it is not for, in the instructions and the name|Restrict access to the people who understand what the Project is suitable for so that inappropriate use does not arise in the first place|Rely on people's own judgement about scope|Monitor how the Project is being used and intervene when someone uses it for something it was not designed to handle appropriately|Making the scope visible in the name and the instructions is what lets people self-select correctly without gatekeeping or monitoring.|Access restriction limits legitimate use. Relying on judgement without stating scope leaves people guessing, and monitoring intervenes after misuse.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|An associate's Project holds documents in three languages and answers are inconsistent in quality between them.|Test performance per language and curate accordingly|Translate all the documents into one language so that the Project's knowledge is consistent and the answers do not vary|Ask questions in the language of the relevant document|Accept the variation, since handling several languages within one body of knowledge is inherently more difficult than working in a single language|Performance varies by language, so measuring it per language is what tells you whether the material needs translating, splitting or leaving alone.|Translating everything is expensive and may be unnecessary. Matching question language is a workaround, and accepting variation skips the measurement.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team's project instruction produces good results for most questions and unhelpful results for one common question type.|Add a scoped rule for that question type rather than changing the general instruction|Rewrite the general instruction so that it produces good results for the problematic question type as well as for everything else it currently handles|Tell people to avoid that type of question|Create a second Project specifically for the question type that the current instructions do not handle well enough for the team's purposes|A scoped addition preserves what already works while handling the exception. Rewriting the general rule risks the majority case.|A general rewrite trades working behaviour for the exception. Avoiding the question type ignores a real need, and a second Project is heavier than a scoped rule.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team's Project contains a document nobody can identify the source or date of.|Establish its provenance or remove it, since unattributable knowledge cannot be trusted|Keep it, since the content appears accurate and removing material that is producing useful answers would reduce the Project's usefulness to the team|Keep it and add a note about the uncertainty|Ask the team whether anyone recognises the document and keep it if nobody objects to it remaining in the Project's knowledge base|Knowledge whose origin cannot be established cannot be verified or maintained. Resolving provenance or removing it is what keeps the Project trustworthy.|Apparent accuracy is not verification. A note documents the problem, and absence of objection is not provenance.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|An associate wants to share a Project's configuration with another team without sharing its documents.|Share the instructions as a template the other team populates with their own material|Share the whole Project and ask the other team not to look at the documents that are not relevant to the work they are doing with it|Describe the configuration to them verbally|Give the other team read access so they can see how it is set up and build their own version based on what they observe about the configuration|Separating the reusable method from the local material is what makes configuration shareable without exposing content.|Sharing everything with a request not to look is not a control. A verbal description is lossy, and read access still exposes the documents.
organisation|5.4|3|A team's Projects each work well individually but there is no record of which ones exist or who uses them.|Maintain a register with owner and purpose for each Project|Ask people to name their Projects clearly so that the purpose of each one is evident from its name|Consolidate the four purposes into fewer Projects|Review the Projects annually so that the team has an up-to-date picture of what exists and who is responsible for each one at that point in time|An inventory with ownership is what makes a growing set of Projects governable, particularly when people leave or Projects fall out of use.|Good naming helps discovery but records neither ownership nor usage. Consolidation destroys isolation, and an annual review is a register maintained slowly.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team's Project performs well on questions about recent documents and poorly on older ones, and all are equally relevant.|Check whether the older documents are formatted differently from the recent ones|Upload the older documents again so that they are processed in the same way as the more recent ones that appear to be working correctly|Reduce the total number of documents held|Ask questions about older material more specifically so that the model is more likely to locate the relevant sections within those documents|A systematic difference between two groups of documents usually reflects a difference in the documents themselves, such as format or structure, which is checkable.|Re-uploading assumes a processing failure without evidence. Reducing documents is blunt, and question specificity works around an unexplained pattern.
projects-setup|5.1|3|An associate is setting up a Project and wants to know whether to include examples of good output.|Include them if the output has a shape that is hard to describe but easy to show|Include them always, since examples are the most reliable way to communicate what is wanted and will improve output|Leave the examples out of the configuration|Include a large number of examples so that the model has as much material as possible to learn the expected pattern from within the configuration|Examples earn their place when the target is easier to demonstrate than to describe. That criterion is what decides whether to include them.|Always including examples spends configuration space regardless of need. Excluding them loses a strong technique, and a large number dilutes other instructions.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project holds a long document and questions concern only its tables, which the answers often get wrong.|Extract the tables into their own document so they are not buried in prose|Ask questions that refer specifically to the tables so that the model knows to look at them|Upload the document a second time to be sure|Add an instruction telling the model that the tables are the most important part of the document and should be consulted before the prose sections|Structured data buried in a long prose document is harder to use. Extracting it into its own document is what makes it directly available.|Question specificity and instructions both work around the structure. Re-uploading does not change how the content is organised.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team's project instructions have never been reviewed and were written by someone who has since changed roles.|Review them against what the Project is currently used for|Leave them, since the Project is producing good answers and changing the instructions risks degrading behaviour that currently works well for everyone|Rewrite the instructions from scratch entirely|Ask the original author whether the instructions are still appropriate for how the Project is being used by the team at the moment|Configuration written for an earlier purpose may not match current use. Reviewing against actual usage is what establishes whether it still fits.|Leaving them unexamined assumes continued fit. Rewriting discards working configuration, and the original author no longer knows current usage.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|An associate wants to add a document to a Project but is not sure whether it duplicates material already there.|Check what is already there before adding, since duplication produces inconsistency|Add it, since additional material can only improve the Project's coverage of the topics the team asks questions about in their work|Add it now and remove any duplicates later on|Add it and rely on the model to reconcile any differences between the new document and the material that is already in the Project|Duplicated or near-duplicated material produces inconsistent answers depending on which copy is drawn on. Checking first prevents that.|Assuming more is better ignores the inconsistency. Deferring cleanup lets the problem operate, and expecting reconciliation asks the model to resolve a conflict it cannot verify.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team's shared Project is producing answers that suit the person who configured it and not the rest of the team.|Gather what the others need and reconfigure against the whole team's use|Ask the other team members to adjust their questions so that they get the answers they need from the current configuration of the Project|Let each person keep their own separate Project|Ask the person who configured it to explain their reasoning|Shared configuration should be built from shared requirements. Gathering what the team needs is what makes it serve the team rather than its author.|Asking users to adapt puts the burden in the wrong place. Individual Projects lose the sharing benefit, and an explanation does not change the configuration.
organisation|5.4|3|A team is deciding whether to keep one Project per document set or one per workflow.|Follow the workflow, since that is what determines which material is needed together|Follow the document sets, since that keeps each Project's knowledge coherent and makes it obvious which documents belong in which Project|Follow whichever of them produces fewer Projects|Follow the document sets initially and reorganise around workflows later|Projects exist to serve work, and a workflow determines which material must be available together. That is the boundary that makes each Project coherent in use.|Document-set boundaries organise the material rather than the work. Minimising Project count is not a design criterion, and planned reorganisation defers the decision.
context-limits|5.4|3|An associate's Project gives shorter answers than it used to, and the only change was adding more documents.|Check whether the added material is competing with what the questions need|Ask for longer answers in the instructions so that the responses return to the length they were before the additional documents were added|Remove whichever documents were most recently added|Accept the change, since answer length is not by itself an indication that the quality of the answers has declined in any meaningful way|A change in behaviour following a change in knowledge points at the added material competing for attention. Checking that is the direct diagnosis.|A length instruction treats the symptom. Removing recent additions may be right but skips the diagnosis, and dismissing the change ignores a signal.
projects-setup|5.1|3|A team wants their Project to be usable by someone who has never used one before.|Configure it so a first-time user needs to supply only the variable input|Write a guide explaining how Projects work so that new users understand the concept before they attempt to use the one the team has set up|Have an experienced user run the Project for them|Add instructions to the Project explaining to the user what they should do|Usability comes from removing what the user must know. If only the variable input is required, prior familiarity stops mattering.|A guide teaches a concept rather than removing the need for it. A proxy user is a bottleneck, and in-Project guidance still requires the user to act on it.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project contains documents that are correct individually but describe processes that changed at different times.|Establish which describes current practice and remove the rest|Keep them all and add an instruction explaining the order in which the processes changed|Keep only the most recent of the three documents|Add dates to each document so that the model can determine which of them describes the most current version of the process being asked about|Overlapping descriptions of a changing process produce inconsistent answers. Identifying current practice and removing the rest is what resolves it.|Chronology instructions and dates both rely on correct inference. Keeping the most recent is close but assumes recency equals currency without checking.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|An associate wants their Project to behave differently depending on who is asking, and everyone shares one configuration.|Recognise that one configuration serves everyone, and split if the needs truly differ|Write instructions describing the different behaviours and ask users to identify themselves at the start of each question they put to the Project|Configure it for the most common kind of user|Add all the different behaviours to the instructions so that the model can choose whichever one seems most appropriate for the question it receives|Standing configuration applies uniformly. If per-user behaviour is genuinely needed, separate Projects is the structural answer rather than a runtime conditional.|Self-identification and model-selected behaviour both depend on correct resolution each time. Configuring for the common user ignores the others.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team's Project knowledge is well curated but nobody has checked whether the documents are still accurate.|Verify the content, since curation and accuracy are different properties|Assume the documents are accurate, since they were selected deliberately and the team has been using the Project|Ask the model to check the documents itself|Re-upload the documents from their original sources so that the Project holds whatever the current version of each of them happens to be|Selecting the right documents does not establish that they are correct. Verification is a separate check from curation.|Absence of noticed problems is weak evidence. Model self-checking has no external reference, and re-uploading refreshes without verifying.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team wants to give a contractor access to a Project for a three-month engagement.|Plan the access removal at the same time as granting it|Grant access and remove it when the engagement ends, which the team will remember to do because the end date is known in advance to everyone involved|Grant the contractor permanent access instead|Give the contractor a copy of the Project so that they have what they need|Time-bounded access should have its removal planned when it is granted, because that is when the end date is known and the intent is clear.|Relying on remembering at the end is how access persists. Permanent access outlives the engagement, and a copy distributes the material without any control at all.
organisation|5.4|3|An associate's Projects work well but they cannot explain to a new team member how the set is organised.|Take the inability to explain it as the signal that the organisation needs work|Explain the Projects individually so the new team member learns what each of them is for as they encounter them during the course of their work|Give them access to everything and let them look|Ask the new team member to work it out over time, since familiarity with how the Projects are organised will develop naturally as they use them|If the scheme cannot be explained, it is not a scheme. That difficulty is the diagnostic rather than a communication problem.|Explaining case by case transfers a list rather than a structure. Broad access and learning by exposure both leave the disorganisation intact.
